# 80 — The review (read-only; you change nothing while reviewing)

Review the task's work as if you did not write it. Read the brief in force (`roadmap.md` line 3 →
`docs/governance/briefs/<file>.md`), these rules, and the whole diff `git diff <BASE>..HEAD`, where
`<BASE>` is the task's `Base: dev <sha>`. Read each base version with `git show <BASE>:<path>`. Report
under these headings, each with file:line evidence, or "none" after naming the grep you ran:

1. SCOPE — every changed file against the task's scope (files, globs or areas) and the standing
   allowance; any file outside; any file the task named that was not changed.
2. BEHAVIOUR CONTRACT — for each touched route, door, screen or function: does anything differ from
   the base beyond the task's numbered changes (status, words, body keys, headers, signatures, order
   of side effects)?
3. BANNED PATTERNS — sentinel defaults on identity, geography or screening paths (`?? 'ET'`,
   `?? guest`, `?? 0`); catch-and-continue or a success-shaped answer on failure; a table or function
   born open (no RLS, a grant to anon / authenticated without its allowlist line); a secret or key in
   any file; status written outside a state machine; a loosened assertion, a timeout raised without a
   named cause, a dial raised for a test; a real row used as a fixture or in a proof; hand-written
   database state; an unnamed package change; a route that parses, authenticates or fetches by its
   own code; a stand-in reachable outside the E2E build; a model answer stored or shown unparsed; a
   side effect before the route's door answered; a hard-coded user-visible string; a literal
   language / country / currency code outside the constants module; a colour, shadow, radius or font
   value outside the tokens; `as unknown as`, `any`, `!`, `@ts-expect-error` or `@ts-ignore`; a code
   comment that exempts one place from a shared mechanism; "nothing else changes" written without the
   census that proves it.
4. ONE MECHANISM PER CONCERN — does the change use the mechanism the register names? Did it add a
   second local version of anything (fetch, bearer, parser, formatter, gate, row style)? List every
   sibling that bypasses the mechanism (K2), in scope or not. Does a new mechanism land with its
   guard, proven on a planted bypass, wired in `wired.test.ts`?
5. TESTS — do new or changed tests prove the claim (a refusal test proves the refused side effect did
   not happen; red-first evidence where a defect was fixed; the premise measured after the fixture is
   in place; `vi.stubEnv` for every key; no real row; twin locators; no `.first()`, no
   `waitForTimeout`, no `test.only`)? Which assertions changed shape, and were they listed?
6. MIGRATIONS — born closed, whole redeclaration from the live definition, closers in-file, self-mark
   above the ledger, proofs on scratch rows that RAISE on failure, `profiles(user_id)` not
   `auth.users`, `home_country_code` on personal tables, the pool-reset-map line.
7. DOCS AND RECORDS — feature doc, changelog line, register row, allowlist lines with reason and
   until, deletions declared, both i18n maps regenerated when a key moved.
8. SECURITY — anything the public repository should not carry before a fix is live; any input not
   validated at the boundary; any provider or Postgres text reaching a client.

Severity per finding: BLOCKER (a law broken), MAJOR (a contract or proof gap), MINOR. Never soften a
finding because the brief asked for the change; the brief can be wrong, and that is a finding too.
End with exactly one line, the last of your answer:

Review: READY · <n> BLOCKER · <n> MAJOR · <n> MINOR · on <sha>

— NOT READY instead of READY when any BLOCKER stands; `<sha>` is `git log -1 --format=%h --abbrev=7
HEAD`, the commit you read.
